Read the theorem as a picture. Near \(c\), the graph of \(g\) lies between the graph of \(f\) (below) and the graph of \(h\) (above). If the lower and upper graphs both head to the same height \(L\) as \(x \to c\), the middle graph has nowhere else to go: it is squeezed to \(L\) as well. Think of a slice of filling between two pieces of bread that are pressed together.
Three conditions must all hold:
1. \(f(x) \le g(x) \le h(x)\) for every \(x\) near \(c\) (the value at \(c\) itself does not matter);
2. \(\lim_{x \to c} f(x) = L\);
3. \(\lim_{x \to c} h(x) = L\), the SAME number \(L\).
